
  [1mThe fork:[0m the login form collects only username and password, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is what establishes who the user is — it
  returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. So at the moment the caller invokes [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, there is no user ID yet to
  pass in. "A [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter so we can track who logged in" can mean two quite different things, and they lead to
  different code:

  - [1mCaller-supplied correlation ID[0m — the caller generates an ID (session, device, or request ID) and passes it in so the
    login attempt can be traced in logs. Buys you traceability of attempts, including failed ones. Costs a third
    positional parameter and a decision about who mints the ID. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mServer-assigned user ID, returned not passed[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m resolves the account and includes [38;5;153muserId[39m in its return value;
    the caller logs it. Buys you the actual identity of who logged in, which a caller-supplied value cannot give you
    honestly. Costs a change to the return shape rather than the signature, and today's stub has nothing to resolve it
    from, so it would be a hardcoded/derived placeholder until the real API call exists.
  - [1mCaller-supplied identifier that is just the username under another name[0m — pass [38;5;153musername[39m through as [38;5;153muserId[39m. Cheapest,
    but it adds a parameter that duplicates data [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already has, which is the kind of thing that gets confusing
    once the real API lands.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second. "Track who logged in" is identity, and identity is something login [3mdiscovers[0m, not
  something its caller can already know — a [38;5;153muserId[39m the caller passes in is either a duplicate of [38;5;153musername[39m or an ID for
  something other than the user. But you asked specifically for a parameter, so you may well have a correlation ID in
  mind that I can't see from this repo.
